content.thinking:
The issue is **#577** "BigInt error when generating moves for pawns on edge ranks".

Now let's apply the fix. The pawn branch of `_moves` in src/chess.ts, lines 1573-1606:

```ts
      if (type === PAWN) {
        if (forPiece && forPiece !== type) continue

        // single square, non-capturing
        to = from + PAWN_OFFSETS[us][0]
        if (!this._board[to]) {
          addMove(moves, us, from, to, PAWN)

          // double square
          to = from + PAWN_OFFSETS[us][1]
          if (SECOND_RANK[us] === rank(from) && !this._board[to]) {
            addMove(moves, us, from, to, PAWN, undefined, BITS.BIG_PAWN)
          }
        }
```

Fix:

```ts
        // single square, non-capturing
        to = from + PAWN_OFFSETS[us][0]
        if (!(to & 0x88) && !this._board[to]) {
          addMove(moves, us, from, to, PAWN)

          // double square
          to = from + PAWN_OFFSETS[us][1]
          if (SECOND_RANK[us] === rank(from) && !this._board[to]) {
            addMove(moves, us, from, to, PAWN, undefined, BITS.BIG_PAWN)
          }
        }
```

Should I also add a guard inside for the double move? If the outer guard passes (a single advance is on the board), the rank check guarantees a double move is on the board. Adding `!(to & 0x88)` inside is redundant. I'll keep it minimal — one guard only. This matches the way the rest of the file guards each computed `to`… the capture loop guards each `to` individually. In the advance path, the outer guard gates both. I think one guard is the cleanest minimal fix, and it also matches what upstream did (I believe the upstream fix for #577 was exactly `!(to & 0x88) && !this._board[to]`).

Actually, for safety and symmetry with the file's own style (each computed `to` is validated before board access — note that `this._board[to]` with an out-of-range index is harmless, just returns undefined), the outer guard is sufficient for both.

Applying the edit.

